    <!-- 4. LEGAL -->
    <section id="legal">
        <div class="section-header" role="button" tabindex="0">
            <h3>Legal & Ethical Boundaries</h3>
            <span class="chevron">▾</span>
        </div>
        <div class="section-body">
            <p class="sub">Indian legal framework for OSINT and security testing. Orientation, not legal advice.</p>

            <div class="panel">
                <h4>DPDP Act, 2023</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>Governs digital personal data processing in India.</li>
                    <li>Requires lawful basis (consent or legitimate use).</li>
                    <li>Penalties up to <strong>₹250 crore</strong>.</li>
                    <li>"Publicly available" data is still covered if processed beyond its original purpose.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="panel">
                <h4>IT Act, 2000 — Key Sections</h4>
                <table class="op-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Section</th><th>Covers</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>43</strong></td><td>Unauthorized access, downloading, damaging resources.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>43A</strong></td><td>Compensation for failing to protect sensitive personal data.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>66</strong></td><td>Computer offences — up to 3 years and/or ₹5 lakh.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>66C</strong></td><td>Identity theft — up to 3 years and/or ₹1 lakh.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>66D</strong></td><td>Cheating by personation.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>66E</strong></td><td>Privacy violation — capturing/publishing private images.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>66F</strong></td><td>Cyber terrorism — up to life.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>72</strong></td><td>Breach of confidentiality by service providers.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="panel">
                <h4>Legal vs. Not</h4>
                <table class="op-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Activity</th><th>Status</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Googling your own name</td><td>Legal</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Dorking your own domain</td><td>Legal</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Dorking a client's domain with signed authorization</td><td>Legal</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Dorking a random company "to see what's there"</td><td>Illegal (IT Act 43/66)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Checking your own email in breaches</td><td>Legal</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Looking up someone else's email in breaches</td><td>Illegal (DPDP + IT Act)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Aggregating a person's data into a profile</td><td>Illegal (DPDP Act)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Port scanning a system you don't own</td><td>Illegal (IT Act 43)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Port scanning your own system</td><td>Legal</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="panel">
                <h4>Responsible Disclosure</h4>
                <ol>
                    <li>Don't exploit further. Stop and document.</li>
                    <li>Report privately — look for <code>/.well-known/security.txt</code>, bug bounty, or security contact.</li>
                    <li>Give reasonable time (typically 90 days).</li>
                    <li>Don't demand payment — that becomes extortion (IT Act 66D).</li>
                    <li>Document timestamps, scope, findings.</li>
                </ol>
                <p>India's national coordinator: <strong>CERT-In</strong> — cert-in.org.in</p>
            </div>

            <div class="panel">
                <h4>Bug Bounty Platforms</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>HackerOne — hackerone.com</li>
                    <li>Bugcrowd — bugcrowd.com</li>
                    <li>Intigriti — intigriti.com</li>
                    <li>YesWeHack — yeswehack.com</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 5. SELF-AUDIT -->
    <section id="selfaudit">
        <div class="section-header" role="button" tabindex="0">
            <h3>Self-Audit Guide</h3>
            <span class="chevron">▾</span>
        </div>
        <div class="section-body">
            <p class="sub">Check what's exposed about you and remove it. Basic digital hygiene.</p>

            <div class="ok">
                <strong>Why:</strong> Most people are surprised by what's publicly visible. Run this quarterly.
            </div>

            <div class="panel">
                <h4>1 — Email Breach Check</h4>
                <ol>
                    <li>Go to <strong>haveibeenpwned.com</strong>.</li>
                    <li>Enter all your emails.</li>
                    <li>For each breach: change password, enable 2FA, stop reusing passwords.</li>
                    <li>Use a password manager (Bitwarden, KeePassXC).</li>
                </ol>
            </div>

            <div class="panel">
                <h4>2 — Google Yourself</h4>
                <pre><code>"your full name"
"your full name" site:linkedin.com
"your email"
"your phone"
"your username"
site:pastebin.com "your email"</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="panel">
                <h4>3 — Check Your Own Domain</h4>
                <pre><code>site:yourdomain.com intitle:"index of"
site:yourdomain.com filetype:env
site:yourdomain.com filetype:sql
site:yourdomain.com inurl:admin</code></pre>
                <p>Also: <strong>crt.sh</strong> for subdomains, <strong>Shodan</strong> for exposed services.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="panel">
                <h4>4 — Social Media Lockdown</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>Review privacy on Facebook, Instagram, X, LinkedIn, TikTok.</li>
                    <li>Remove phone/email from public profiles.</li>
                    <li>Turn off "searchable by phone/email."</li>
                    <li>Audit old posts with location data.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="panel">
                <h4>5 — Data Broker Removal</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>India: Justdial, IndiaMART, people-search sites.</li>
                    <li>Global: Spokeo, Whitepages, BeenVerified.</li>
                    <li>Use opt-out forms. Document. Re-check quarterly.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="panel">
                <h4>6 — Account Hygiene</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>Password manager: Bitwarden, KeePassXC.</li>
                    <li>2FA app: Aegis, Ente Auth. Or hardware key.</li>
                    <li>Email aliases: SimpleLogin, Firefox Relay.</li>
                    <li>Delete old accounts: justdelete.me.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="panel">
                <h4>7 — Removal Requests</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Google:</strong> support.google.com/websearch/troubleshooter/3111061</li>
                    <li><strong>India:</strong> cybercrime.gov.in</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 6. PENTEST -->
    <section id="pentest">
        <div class="section-header" role="button" tabindex="0">
            <h3>Authorized Pentest Workflow</h3>
            <span class="chevron">▾</span>
        </div>
        <div class="section-body">
            <p class="sub">Professional process for OSINT and dorking under signed authorization.</p>

            <div class="panel">
                <h4>1 — Scope Definition</h4>
                <p>In writing, before touching anything:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>In-scope domains and IPs (exact list).</li>
                    <li>Out-of-scope assets (explicit exclusions).</li>
                    <li>Testing window.</li>
                    <li>Allowed techniques (passive? active? exploitation?).</li>
                    <li>Prohibited actions (DoS, exfiltration, social engineering unless authorized).</li>
                    <li>Emergency contact.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="panel">
                <h4>2 — Authorization & Consent</h4>
                <p>A signed document is non-negotiable:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Client legal name + signatory authority.</li>
                    <li>Exact scope.</li>
                    <li>Testing window.</li>
                    <li>Statement of ownership/authority.</li>
                    <li>Liability limitation and indemnification.</li>
                    <li>Data handling agreement.</li>
                    <li>NDA if separate.</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="warn">
                    <strong>No signature = no testing.</strong> Verbal "go ahead" is not authorization.
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="panel">
                <h4>3 — Passive Recon (OSINT)</h4>
                <p>No direct contact with target systems. Public sources only:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Google dorking on in-scope domains.</li>
                    <li><code>crt.sh</code>, SecurityTrails for certificate transparency.</li>
                    <li>Shodan, Censys for exposed services (read-only queries).</li>
                    <li>WHOIS, DNS history.</li>
                    <li>Social media and public filings.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="panel">
                <h4>4 — Active Recon
